A||||14||v8vinnie||REMOVIL OF STEERING SHOCK||||1||
Z||000000||v8vinnie||01-06-2001||09:24 PM||||okay guys hers another one for you. Should i remove the steering shock ( dampener) as per herb addams sugestion,or leave it on. He says to do this if you are going to put bigger sway bars on the front,any body done this, any good results?. My shock leaks a little any way so i was woundering if i should bother replacing it, any thoughts?.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||01-06-2001||09:52 PM||||It probably depends on the wheels and tires you are using in front, and how your front suspension is modified and aligned, and how much you want to wrestle with the steering wheel.<P>I removed the steering damper from my '85 SE with stock alloy wheels and 215/60-14 tires.  Without the damper, transient steering was quicker and lighter; however, the steering tended to follow road irregularities -- rough or grooved pavement -- particularly under braking.  Later, I installed identical-looking Grand Am wheels with 13mm greater offset on the front, moving the tires inboard and reducing the scrub radius.  Now, transient and steady-state steering are both lighter, and the car is more stable on rough pavement.  At speed, steering is almost too light.  I may need more caster.<P>With wide tires, you may need the damper.  Try removing it; if you don't like the feel, it's easy to re-install.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||v8vinnie||01-06-2001||09:58 PM||||GOT IT !!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||01-07-2001||09:13 AM||||Also it is possible that removing the steering damper and driving on rough roads often/daily can cause extra wear on tierod ends. It all depends on suspension set-up and where you drive it. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||01-07-2001||09:33 AM||||Your damper may already be shot as it was on my 87 coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||01-07-2001||10:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Also it is possible that removing the steering damper and driving on rough roads often/daily can cause extra wear on tierod ends. It all depends on suspension set-up and where you drive it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Think about this for a minute... What forces will the tie rod ends experience without the damper which they would not experience with the damper? NONE. Removing the damper will not affect tie rod end life.<P>I'm also betting your damper is already shot.<P>Another thing to consider is that going to <I>taller</I> tires will reduce your scrub radius just as going to wheels with more offset. I am running 235/60-15's in front, and the steering is significantly lighter than with the stock sizes. They rub a tiny bit at large stering angles, but it's nothing to worry about.<P>MrPBody: What year GA did your wheels come from?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||01-08-2001||02:16 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> Think about this for a minute... What forces will the tie rod ends experience without the damper which they would not experience with the damper? NONE. Removing the damper will not affect tie rod end life.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Wrong.<BR>The steering damper helps absorb shock that the rack would see. This is done by decreasing the rate of travel when the rack is forced hard by a pothole for example. The weakest point of the steering is the tie rod ends since they are at the end of the rack. Think about the thing with the steel balls for desks, a ball hits one side and the force is transmitted thru a few steel balls to the other end ball with equal force. If a damper was installed the force transmitted thru would be far less. Also provide better control over uneven surfaces as noted above.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||01-08-2001||08:07 AM||||I've removed mine from the SE the GT and a couple Ga. Fieros members cars... makes the sttering lighter.. yeah it can be annoying on REALLY bumpy roads but not enough to replace it.. just ditch it and run. been off the SE since 9-99 off the GT since 7-00 and others in between all the other owners are happy with the removal.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||01-08-2001||10:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Wrong.<BR>The steering damper helps absorb shock that the rack would see. This is done by decreasing the rate of travel when the rack is forced hard by a pothole for example. The weakest point of the steering is the tie rod ends since they are at the end of the rack. Think about the thing with the steel balls for desks, a ball hits one side and the force is transmitted thru a few steel balls to the other end ball with equal force. If a damper was installed the force transmitted thru would be far less. Also provide better control over uneven surfaces as noted above.<P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-08-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the right tire has a force exerted on it, there will be a torque around the steering axis. This will result in a force through the right outer and inner tie rod ends into the rack. This force on the tie rod ends will be the same whether the force on the rack is absorbed by the driver at the steering wheel or by the steering damper.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||01-08-2001||11:14 AM||||Yes the force will be transmitted into the rack and continue to the left side. There by applying unneccesary force to the left side. There isn't a driver out there who is strong and quick enough to dampen the shock of a 2700 pound car hitting a pothole. Also that force is applied to the steering gear inside the rack which goes to the steering wheel. Like those above who stated the steering feels lighter/faster. So when force is exerted thru the steering the same force is applied to the steering wheel at the same speed and pretty close to the same force. So the chance of over correcting goes up.<BR>I'm not saying it'll fail a month down the road but over time it will apply undo stress to many parts. Tierods are just one of the parts. The rack end seals (which are nylon originally) are another.<BR>Force/torque does not stop at the rack, it has to be dampened or absorbed. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||01-08-2001||01:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Yes the force will be transmitted into the rack and continue to the left side. There by applying unneccesary force to the left side. There isn't a driver out there who is strong and quick enough to dampen the shock of a 2700 pound car hitting a pothole. Also that force is applied to the steering gear inside the rack which goes to the steering wheel. Like those above who stated the steering feels lighter/faster. So when force is exerted thru the steering the same force is applied to the steering wheel at the same speed and pretty close to the same force. So the chance of over correcting goes up.<BR>I'm not saying it'll fail a month down the road but over time it will apply undo stress to many parts. Tierods are just one of the parts. The rack end seals (which are nylon originally) are another.<BR>Force/torque does not stop at the rack, it has to be dampened or absorbed. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What, exactly, is the transmitted force pushing against on the other side? Only the left tire's resistance to steering inputs, which is hardly a load of uncommon magnitude.<BR>The damper doesn't react instantly, either. Have you ever looked at it? It has rubber bushings on the ends.<P>Look at the angle the tie rods make with the axis of the rack. Very little of the force exerted on the tie rods is taken by the rack bearings. There's nothing wrong with these bearings being nylon. Nylon isn't weak because it's not metal. A nylon bearing has better NVH characteristics than a metal bearing would, and wears quite well.<P>You are confusing force and motion. A damper ONLY responds to motion; it exerts a force that opposes the motion (specifically, the force is roughly proportional to the speed of motion). In a long sweeping turn the driver is taking the steering force almost entirely at the wheel without the damper taking any of it because there is very little motion in this case, even though there is force on the rack. And in a sweeping left turn, what kind of force is on the left tie rod? Tensile of compressive?<P>Overcorrection is not a probablity. Overcorrection is a result of driver skill or lack thereof and familiarity with the car or lack thereof.<P>I defy you to find any difference in the lifespan of tie rod ends which can be statistically correlated to the car being equiped with a steering damper.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Bigfieroman||01-08-2001||01:14 PM||||Well said!<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||01-08-2001||01:21 PM||||I never said the nylon end bushings were bad because they were nylon. Just that they were nylon. They are end bushings not bearings. They can still be damaged wether they are metal or nylon. The dampener responds to the speed of motion which the faster it moves it increases force. The shaft that goes thru the rack is a solid piece.<BR>I am also talking about road debris and potholes which jars/jolts the steering not sweeping turns. <BR>Overcorrection with a lack of familiarity is true. Removing the dampener changes familiarity. <P>So GM put the dampener on for looks? Power steering racks have reduced valving because they thought it would be neat to do? <P>I stated that it has a <B> POSSIBILTY </B> of causing problems. So to each his own.<BR>It's your car do what you want.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrPBody||01-08-2001||02:33 PM||||Will --<P>I found GA wheels at a local self-service salvage yard, about $25 each.<BR>||||reg||1
